cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use these measurements or tools, please cite them as below."
title: "Moki&Julio: Kinect v1 as a measuring instrument - the depth lattice, the three noise populations, and the Xbox 360 sensor's capabilities"
authors:
  - name: "Moki&Julio"
version: "2.1"
date-released: 2026-09-27
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Hashirama1337s/kinect-v1-depth-levels"
keywords:
  - Kinect
  - Kinect v1
  - Xbox 360 Kinect
  - structured light
  - depth sensor
  - depth quantisation
  - sensor noise
  - metrology
  - PrimeSense
  - calibration
abstract: >-
  Measurements of one Xbox 360 Kinect (PrimeSense PS1080) through Kinect for Windows SDK 1.8 with a direct ctypes/COM binding.
  The SDK's depth output is exactly PrimeSense's published shift-to-depth table at phase 0 (259 values between 1 and 4 m, 345 in the
  0.8-4 m window, matching a 2014 census), whereas libfreenect and OpenNI sit at phase 0.5, so the drivers' value sets interleave by
  half a step. The SDK's calibration blob, read through documented calls only, contains the PrimeSense registration block; the SDK uses
  it only for depth-to-colour registration (mirrored, parallax evaluated at min(z, 2047 mm)), while its 3-D coordinates use a nominal
  pinhole. Also: per-pixel temporal noise as three range-dependent populations (the worst 1 % of pixels carry 95 % of the variance); a
  short-range temporal correlation (detrended ACF ~0.15 at lags 1-8, gone by ~3 s); warm-up drift; capability confirmations (an
  un-demosaiced GRBG 1280x960 Bayer mosaic at 10.22 fps; 10-bit IR at 640x480 via the SDK; near mode, projector-off and colour-camera
  settings unavailable on Xbox hardware; extended depth below the 800 mm clamp; 4-channel audio; accelerometer counts/819); and a
  lateral-scale check against a factory-sized reference (+0.45 % to +1.74 %; no verdict by its sealed rule). Scripts, retractions and
  controls included.
